\hypertarget{nanite-the-next-step-towards-agi-a-monolithic-runtime-neuro-symbolic-transformer-with-an-executable-concept-graph}{%
\section{Nanite: Toward AGI with a Neuro-Symbolic Transformer and Executable Concept Graph on Consumer Hardware}\label{nanite-the-next-step-towards-agi-a-monolithic-runtime-neuro-symbolic-transformer-with-an-executable-concept-graph}}
\hypertarget{abstract}{%
\subsection{Abstract}\label{abstract}}

This paper presents Nanite, a highly unconventional complex-valued neuro-symbolic
transformer that achieves unprecedented efficiency on constrained hardware. The model 
demonstrates remarkable performance in solving the 3D time-independent Schrödinger equation
with 0.03 loss while consuming just 300-500MB memory on consumer hardware 
(2.5GHz Core i5, integrated graphics). In its most compact form, the model reaches 1.8MB
while maintaining a record 0.000038 loss on the 1D case - demonstrating that AGI-esque
capabilities don't necessarily require datacenter-scale resources.

The work presents a radical departure from conventional deep learning
architectures through three key innovations: (1) a complex-valued
monolithic neuro-symbolic design leveraging geometric algebra
principles, (2) an InfiniToeplitz attention mechanism enabling
infinite-context reasoning via structured matrices, and (3) an
executable concept graph that dynamically compresses knowledge
representation and compiles to bytecode. Together, these allow Nanite
to function as both a powerful Physics-Informed Neural Network (quantum
simulations, signal processing) and a general-purpose neuro-symbolic
system.

While current resource constraints limit comprehensive benchmarking, the
demonstrated results on quantum mechanical problems - achieved without
pretraining or large datasets - suggest transformative potential across
scientific computing, edge AI, and beyond. More than just a model,
Nanite represents a provocation: what becomes possible when we rebuild
deep learning from first principles, embracing complex numbers, symbolic
reasoning, and systems-level optimizations?

\hypertarget{introduction}{%
\subsection{Introduction}\label{introduction}}

The field of artificial intelligence stands at a crossroads, where the pursuit of increasingly larger models has led to unprecedented capabilities but also raised concerns about accessibility, efficiency, and environmental impact. This paper introduces Nanite, a novel approach that challenges conventional wisdom by demonstrating that sophisticated AI capabilities can be achieved through architectural innovation rather than scale.

Nanite's main motivation is to explore the boundaries of transformer architectures and investigate how systems programming and low-level development can drive performance and efficiency in AI models. By harmonizing neural architecture, symbolic reasoning, and systems design, this work demonstrates that the future of AI may lie in breaking known assumptions and rethinking deep learning architecture altogether.

